<h2 class="bde-heading-390-178 bde-heading">
Where Do the Bulls Sit Against the Salary Cap?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-211 bde-shortcode"><p>Chicago Bulls salaries for 2026–27 add up to a cap payroll of <strong>$165.77 million</strong>, putting the club <strong>$809,083</strong> above the <strong>$164.96 million</strong> salary cap. That figure includes <strong>$1.08 million</strong> in dead cap on top of a standard roster of <strong>$164.69 million</strong>.</p>
<p>Josh Giddey tops the payroll at <strong>$25.00 million</strong>, ahead of Nic Claxton on <strong>$23.32 million</strong> and Norman Powell on <strong>$21.50 million</strong>, together about <strong>42.4%</strong> of the roster total. Their tax salary is <strong>$34.66 million</strong> below the <strong>$200.43 million</strong> luxury-tax line and apron salary sits <strong>$40.93 million</strong> under the <strong>$209.02 million</strong> first apron.</p>
</div><div class="bde-shortcode-390-179 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-cap" style="--nst-accent:#CE1141"><p>The Chicago Bulls standard roster accounts for $164.69M. Add $1.08M in dead cap to reach $165.77M before cap holds. Holds are separate accounting charges, not signed salary payments.</p><div class="nst-threshold-grid"><div><h3>Salary Cap</h3><strong>$164.96M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:100%"></i></div><p>$0.81M above<br><small>Using cap payroll before holds</small></p></div><div><h3>Luxury-Tax Line</h3><strong>$200.43M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:82.708046280959%"></i></div><p>$34.66M below<br><small>Using tax salary</small></p></div><div><h3>First Apron</h3><strong>$209.02M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:80.417769059637%"></i></div><p>$40.93M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div><div><h3>Second Apron</h3><strong>$221.69M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:75.821296789152%"></i></div><p>$53.60M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div></div><div class="nst-tax-panel"><h3>Estimated Luxury-Tax Bill</h3><div class="nst-tax-summary"><strong>$0.00M</strong><span>Standard tax rates</span></div><p>The recorded tax salary is below the tax line, so this snapshot produces no luxury-tax bill. This is an estimate, not the final season-end assessment.</p><div class="nst-tax-history"><span><b>2022–23</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2023–24</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2024–25</b>Below tax</span><span><b>2025–26</b>Below tax</span></div><p class="nst-note">Three or more taxpayer seasons in the previous four trigger repeater rates. Status reviewed 2026-09-16.</p></div><h3>What Does Going Over the Tax Line Mean?</h3><p>The luxury tax is an additional team expense. It rises in bands as tax salary moves above the threshold; teams with a recent history of paying tax can face higher repeater rates. The final bill depends on the season-end calculation.</p><h3>Why the Aprons Matter</h3><p>Aprons affect how a team can build its roster, including certain exceptions and trades. Being below an apron does not automatically create cap space, and a payroll figure alone does not establish whether a hard cap has been triggered.</p><p class="nst-note">Cap holds: $0.00M. Threshold comparisons describe the recorded salary totals; they do not establish available signing room.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-eras" class="bde-fancy-container-390-172 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-182 bde-heading">
The Chicago Bulls’ Payroll Through the Eras
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-183 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-eras" style="--nst-accent:#CE1141"><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>1966 · The Bulls Are Born</summary><p>Chicago’s Bulls franchise began in 1966.</p></details><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>1991–98 · Six Championships</summary><p>Chicago won NBA titles in 1991, 1992, 1993, 1996, 1997 and 1998.</p></details><h3>The Payroll Over Time</h3><p>Across the four completed seasons shown, cap payroll ranged from $149.42M to $182.88M. The current roster puts 42.39% of active cap payroll into its three largest contracts.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-contracts" class="bde-fancy-container-390-173 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-184 bde-heading">
Bulls Contracts and Future Salaries
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-212 bde-shortcode"><p>Bulls contracts and future salaries center on Giddey, whose <strong>$100.00 million</strong> agreement runs to <strong>2028–29</strong>. Claxton and Powell are both signed through 2027–28, at <strong>$97.00 million</strong> and <strong>$44.08 million</strong> in total value, and Buddy Hield, acquired from Charlotte in September, is signed through 2027–28 with a player option.</p>
<p>Three deals expire after this season: Isaac Okoro, Jalen Smith and Leonard Miller. The furthest commitments belong to Caleb Wilson and Dailyn Swain, both on the books to 2029–30.</p>
<p>For each player’s 2026–27 figure, see the full list of Chicago Bulls salaries in the <a href="#team-roster">roster table</a>.</p>
